    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Direct sentences can sound hard: <em>Give me a coffee! I want a table.</em> Polite sentences sound friendly: <em>Could I have a coffee, please? I'd like a table, please.</em> Use them in shops, cafes, at work and with people you don't know.",
        "tag": "Direct or polite"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Can I…?</strong> and <strong>Could I…?</strong> ask for something or ask to do something. <em>Can I sit here? Could I have the bill, please?</em> <em>Could</em> is a little more polite than <em>can</em>. Both are fine in everyday life.",
        "tag": "Can I / Could I"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Can you…? Could you…? Would you…?</strong> ask someone to do something. Use the base verb: <em>Could you help me, please? Would you open the door?</em> No <em>to</em> after <em>you</em>.",
        "tag": "Can you / Could you / Would you"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>I'd like</strong> (= I would like) is a polite way to say what you want: <em>I'd like a tea. I'd like to book a table.</em> To offer something, ask <strong>Would you like…?</strong>: <em>Would you like some water? Would you like to sit down?</em>",
        "tag": "I'd like / Would you like"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Would you mind + -ing?</strong> is very polite: <em>Would you mind waiting a moment? Would you mind opening the window?</em> To say yes, answer <em>Not at all</em> or <em>Of course not</em>. To say no, use <em>I'm afraid I can't</em>.",
        "tag": "Would you mind…?"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>Could you to help me?</em> → ✓ <em>Could you help me?</em> · ✗ <em>Would you mind to wait?</em> → ✓ <em>Would you mind waiting?</em> · ✗ <em>I want a coffee.</em> (too direct) → ✓ <em>I'd like a coffee, please.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "<em>Could</em> and <em>would</em> look like past forms, but here they are not about the past. They just make a request softer. With friends, <em>Can you…?</em> is fine. With strangers or in a formal place, choose <em>Could you…?</em> or <em>Would you mind…?</em> And add <em>please</em>. After <em>can</em> and <em>could</em>, the verb is always the base verb: see <a href=\"/modal-verbs\">Modal verbs</a>. A plain command like <em>Open the window.</em> can sound rude. Compare <a href=\"/imperatives\">Imperatives</a>.",
    "reference": {
